  "episodeId": "demo-one-person-company",
  "title": "The One-Person Company",
  "topic": "AI agents, leverage, and the future of work",
  "durationSeconds": 90,
  "fps": 30,
  "themeId": "MIDNIGHT_CYAN",
  "narration": {
    "text": "Imagine it's 2028. A startup launches with one employee. You.\n\nNo team. No office. Just a laptop, a strong idea, and an army of AI agents working while you sleep.\n\nThey answer support tickets, write the code, chase invoices, and ship the updates. By morning, your company has done a week of work.\n\nAt first, it feels like magic. Then it feels like leverage. One person, doing what used to take fifty. A solo founder with a clear system can now ship like a team of twenty, and pivot before a committee finishes its first meeting.\n\nThe tools are already here. The agents are getting better every month. The cost of starting is collapsing. And the old advantage, headcount, is becoming a liability. That doesn't make people worthless. It makes coordination cheap, and judgment scarce.\n\nBut here's the part nobody puts in the demo.\n\nAutomation can multiply a good decision. It can also multiply a bad one, faster than anyone can catch it. One careless prompt can become a thousand bad emails, or a headline.\n\nSo the skill that matters isn't typing prompts. It's knowing which decisions should never be delegated.\n\nThe one-person company is coming. The question is not whether you can run it alone. It's whether you'll know what still needs a human. Not because you can do everything. Because you know what not to automate.\n\nThat's the future worth building.",
